    "created_at": "2016-03-23T16:08:41Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-03-23T16:08:41Z",
    "body": "I don't think we should uses microtasks simply because the microtask checkpoint is not at a stage of the event loop processing model where we are guaranteed to have a completely drawn frame in the canvas. The microtask checkpoint is after regular task execution and before requestAnimationFrame callbacks are processed, which is awkward.\n\nThe fact that a lot of media processing does not happen on the same thread as script execution is probably not an issue for the spec to worry about. I think it is fine to describe media capture as a step of the single-threaded browsing context event loop. The constraint on threaded implementations is that they must produce equivalent results.  Currently, canvas animation frame boundaries are defined by the completion of an event loop iteration that touches the contents of the canvas. Step 7.12 of the event loop processing model is where the frame boundary is at.\n\nSee: https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#event-loop\n\nI would like to propose a different approach to spec'ing this.\nWe'd add a step right before (or right after?) step 7.12 of the event loop processing model that says something like: \"For each fully active Document in <var>docs</var>, run the \"process frame capture requests\" steps for that Document, passing in <var>now</var> as the timestamp.\"\n\nThen, the \"process frame capture requests\" steps would state that for each element in the document that has frameCaptureRequested set to true, capture a frame if the element's pixel contents have changed since the previous frame capture or if there was no previous frame capture.\n\nNote: if the event loop runs at a rate that is higher than the capture stream's maximum frame rate, frames can be dropped.\n\nAlso, I don't think there is any need to tie captures to commits.  Doing so would be an unnecessary synchronization constraint. Not to mention that canvases that are not attached to the DOM don't get committed, but they should still be capturable. Synchronization between capture and display would happen naturally anyways in cases where the animation is driven by requestAnimationFrame.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
