    "created_at": "2016-03-24T15:44:33Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-03-24T15:44:33Z",
    "body": "Yeah, the implementation in Blink does not use an enumeration of nodes in a particular document. It just does a DOM traversal. When a canvas's contents are touched, the element is marked in a way that indicates that it requires a \"paint invalidation\". In what we call the \"paint invalidation step\" all the elements that are so marked get processed for a graphics update, that is when we invoke the stream capture.  In its current state, Blink's implementation will not call the stream capture listeners for canvases that are not attached to the DOM, but that should probably be fixed. The fix would likely involve having a dedicated stage in the event loop, instead of coupling the media stream update to paint invalidation. The mechanics would be similar though: when a canvas that is being watched by a media stream is drawn to, it would register itself in a list of canvases whose frame needs to be captured. This list would probably live in the Document object. When the event loop reaches the \"process frame capture requests\" step, that's when we'd call HTMLCanvasElement::notifyListenersCanvasChanged.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
